---
import Callout from '@robyrew/ui/components/Callout.astro';
import PageHead from '@robyrew/ui/components/PageHead.astro';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const repository = 'https://github.com/RobyRew/spotify-versions-history';
---

<Base section="about" title="About" description="How the archive finds Spotify's builds, how it checks each file, which identities it pins, and what it never does.">
  <PageHead eyebrow="About" title="How the archive works" icon="info"
    lead="Probes read the same channels Spotify’s own clients and the stores use; every file is checked against its publisher’s signature before it is offered." />

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Where builds come from</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Windows and macOS:</strong> Spotify’s permanent download URLs on download.scdn.co, which serve the current build, and Spotify’s own update service.</li>
      <li><strong>Linux:</strong> Spotify’s signed apt repository and the Snap Store, which keeps every revision downloadable.</li>
      <li><strong>Microsoft Store:</strong> the Store’s catalog and Windows Update’s delivery service, the way a Windows PC downloads the app.</li>
      <li><strong>Older builds:</strong> amd64fox’s LoadSpot table and mirror, offered only when no Spotify or store link is alive.</li>
      <li><strong>iPhone, iPad and Android:</strong> the App Store and Google Play listings; App Store files are tied to the account that downloads them and are not archived.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="verify">How a file is checked</h2>
    <p>
      Each file is downloaded and hashed in one pass, compared with every hash its channel states, and checked against its
      platform’s signature: Authenticode for Windows installers, Microsoft’s package signature for Store packages (with
      every block of every file it contains), codesign, Gatekeeper and the stapled notarization ticket for macOS, the signed
      apt index for Debian packages, and Canonical’s signed assertions for snaps. The archive’s own code checks each one,
      and the platform’s tools check it again on Linux, macOS and Windows machines; all of them must agree on the bytes.
    </p>
    <p>
      The identities a build must carry — Spotify’s Windows signer, Apple team, apt key and Store publisher, and the root
      certificates they chain to — are written down in the repository with where and when they were read. A changed
      identity fails the checks until someone reviews it. The details, and the commands to repeat every check by hand, are
      in <a href={`${repository}/blob/main/docs/VERIFICATION.md`}>docs/VERIFICATION.md</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>What it never does</h2>
    <Callout tone="warn" title="Official, unmodified builds only">
      The archive does not host or link modified clients, patches or cracks, and is not affiliated with Spotify. A file is
      offered only as its publisher signed it.
    </Callout>

    <h2>Questions and removal requests</h2>
    <p>
      Open an issue in the <a href={`${repository}/issues`}>repository</a>. A file named in a request is disabled while it is
      reviewed; its record and hashes stay.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .prose {
    max-width: 46rem;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
    color: var(--ink-2);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
  .prose h2 {
    margin: 2rem 0 0.6rem;
    color: var(--ink);
    font-size: 1.15rem;
  }
  .prose ul {
    padding-left: 1.1rem;
  }
  .prose li {
    margin: 0.35rem 0;
  }
  .prose a {
    color: var(--accent);
  }
</style>
